### From perfect OCR to faithful Markdown

I think this is a much more interesting problem than OCR itself.

My central recommendation is:

Don't convert OCR output directly into Markdown. Reconstruct the document first, then render that reconstruction into Markdown.

That distinction is fundamental.

Imagine a scanned page containing:

* A section heading.

* Two columns of text.

* A photograph with a caption.

* A table with three columns.

* A footnote.

* A paragraph that continues onto the next page.

Perfect OCR gives you the characters and words. It doesn't necessarily tell you how these elements relate to each other.

Even with 100% word recognition, you could produce a Markdown file that is materially misleading because the heading is at the wrong level, the caption is detached from its image, the footnote is placed in the middle of a paragraph, or the table has lost its row and column relationships.

I think this is the next major architectural opportunity for Wiseau.

### 1\. What the academic literature is telling us

There has been a shift in document-processing research away from simply extracting text and towards reconstructing the logical structure of an entire document.

Three research developments are particularly relevant.

OmniDocBench (CVPR 2025)

This benchmark evaluates document parsing across text, tables, mathematical formulas, layout and reading order. Importantly, it contains annotations for different kinds of document components and their relationships, not just their textual contents.

MPDocBench-Parse (2026)

This is particularly relevant to what you're trying to achieve. It evaluates multi-page documents rather than treating each PDF page as an isolated object.

Its authors explicitly identify shortcomings in existing systems around:

* Connecting paragraphs and tables that continue across pages.

* Recovering heading hierarchies.

* Preserving and relating figures and their captions.

* Reconstructing logical reading order.

* Preserving document-level continuity.

It contains 433 manually annotated documents and 3,246 pages. GitHub +1

LongDocBench (2026)

This takes the problem a step further by looking at relationships across lengthy documents.

It investigates recovering the table-of-contents hierarchy and relationships between figures, tables, captions, notes and sources. This is important because identifying individual elements isn't sufficient: you need to know which elements belong together. arxiv.org

The common message is that document conversion needs to recover both content and structure.

That is precisely the distinction I would build Wiseau around.

### 2\. The architectural change I'd make to Wiseau

I'd introduce an intermediate representation (IR): a structured description of the document that exists independently of the Markdown output.

This is an established architectural approach in document-processing systems.

The most relevant example is actually Docling itself.

Its `DoclingDocument` model separates the document's content from its organisation:

* `texts`: paragraphs, section headings, equations and other text.

* `tables`: tables with their structural annotations.

* `pictures`: images and associated information.

* `body`: the hierarchical tree of document content.

* `furniture`: headers, footers and other page furniture.

* `groups`: structural groupings such as lists and chapters.

Items can refer to their parents and children, and the document tree records reading order. GitHub +1

This is much closer to the representation Wiseau ultimately needs than a long Markdown string.

### What I'd have the intermediate representation capture

I'd build around two separate concepts.

A. Document elements

Each element represents something that physically or logically exists in the source.

| Element         | Example                                     |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Document title  | Annual Report 2025                          |
| Section heading | 2.3 Research and Development                |
| Paragraph       | A block of continuous prose                 |
| List            | An ordered or unordered collection          |
| List item       | One entry within a list                     |
| Table           | A structured collection of rows and columns |
| Figure          | A photograph, illustration or chart         |
| Caption         | The text belonging to a figure or table     |
| Footnote        | A note associated with a reference          |
| Equation        | A mathematical expression                   |
| Header/footer   | Repeated page furniture                     |
| Page number     | Original printed pagination                 |
| Code block      | A code sample or extract                    |

B. Relationships between those elements

This is the part I think is especially important.

A document isn't just a collection of blocks. The blocks have relationships.

For example:

| Relationship    | Meaning                                        |
| --------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| parent_of       | A section heading contains several subsections |
| caption_of      | A caption belongs to a particular image        |
| footnote_for    | A footnote explains a particular reference     |
| continues_from  | A paragraph continues onto the next page       |
| continues_table | A table continues onto the following page      |
| references      | A paragraph refers to Figure 4                 |
| source_for      | A source note belongs to a particular table    |

LongDocBench is particularly interesting here. Its researchers explicitly annotate these kinds of relationships and found that tables and figures frequently have multiple associated notes, captions and sources. arxiv.org +1

